sam: Quick ask — can someone paste a tiny Go SDK example for generator + streaming that shows the recommended context/cancellation pattern? I'm seeing goroutines stick around when I cancel mid-stream and also a weird auth-refresh race when tokens expire mid-request.

maria: yep, we should add a short recipe to the docs. do you have a minimal repro or is this a conceptual ask? :eyes: 

sam: conceptual for now. simplified flow: start generator stream, read tokens in a goroutine, auth refresh may kick in (background), user cancels request ctx -> want everything cleaned up (stream closed, goroutines exit). right now Recv() seems to block and our read loop never returns.

aj: common mistakes: not wiring the recv loop to ctx.Done() and not closing the stream. Recv should be interruptible by the ctx used to create the stream; if not, call Close/Cancel on the stream to unblock. example pattern: 
```go
ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(parentCtx, 30*time.Second)
defer cancel()

stream, err := client.GeneratorStream(ctx, req)
if err != nil { return err }

done := make(chan struct{})
go func() {
  defer close(done)
  for {
    tok, err := stream.Recv()
    if err != nil {
      // stream ended or error, ensure we exit
      return
    }
    select {
    case <-ctx.Done():
      // request canceled, stop processing tokens
      return
    default:
      handleToken(tok)
    }
  }
}()

// elsewhere: when you want to cancel early
cancel() // will cause ctx.Done() -> recv loop sees it (assuming Recv respects ctx)
// if Recv doesn't return promptly, also call stream.Close() or stream.Cancel() to force unblocking
<-done
```
aj: note: make sure our SDK's Recv implementation respects the ctx passed at creation. if it doesn't, the Close/Cancel helpers must close the underlying transport so Recv unblocks.

kevin: +1. small ergonomics suggestion: expose both Close() and Cancel() on stream. Close is polite shutdown, Cancel is forceful (cancels HTTP request & background retries). docs should recommend: create stream tied to ctx, use context.WithTimeout for deadlines, and always defer a Cancel or Close in calling code.

maria: about auth refresh: don't perform long network refreshes on the generator's request ctx. that ties the token-refresh to the lifetime of the streaming request and can cause surprising cancellations. instead:
- keep a short-lived child ctx for refresh ops with its own timeout
- refresh proactively when token TTL is low, not reactively mid-stream if possible
- if you must refresh mid-stream, let the refresh retry/backoff live in a separate goroutine and apply the new token for subsequent requests (or trigger a graceful reconnect)

sam: that helps. one subtlety: if the refresh goroutine needs user credentials from a prompt (rare for server flows) that could block everything — better to fail fast and surface an error rather than hang the stream.

devex-bot: @sam we've got a draft PR in docs: docs/devex/streaming-cancellation.md with a sample like the one above. i'll link it in the onboarding checklist and add a small note about auth refresh patterns.

aj: here's a compact helper wrapper idea for ergonomics (pseudo):
```go
// StreamRunner wraps a generator stream and ensures context-bound shutdown
type StreamRunner struct {
  stream *gen.Stream
  done chan struct{}
}

func NewStreamRunner(ctx context.Context, client *gen.Client, req gen.Request) (*StreamRunner, error) {
  stream, err := client.GeneratorStream(ctx, req)
  if err != nil { return nil, err }
  sr := &StreamRunner{stream: stream, done: make(chan struct{})}
  go func() {
    defer close(sr.done)
    for {
      tok, err := stream.Recv()
      if err != nil { return }
      select {
      case <-ctx.Done(): return
      default: process(tok)
      }
    }
  }()
  return sr, nil
}

func (sr *StreamRunner) Wait() { <-sr.done }
func (sr *StreamRunner) Cancel() { sr.stream.Cancel() }
```
aj: this keeps the common wiring in one place so app code is simpler.

kevin: small doc nit — call out that deadlines (WithTimeout) are preferred for user-facing requests, but Cancel() should be used for immediate shutdowns from admin flows. also show an example where Recv returns io.EOF and you treat it as normal completion.

sam: awesome, thanks. i'll try wiring Cancel()+ctx.Done() and make sure our auth refresh uses a dedicated ctx. if i still hit a leak i'll paste a tiny repro and open a bug. :thumbsup:
